# Exercise: The dip Combinator
#
# dip hides the top stack value, runs a quotation, then restores it.
#
# Stack effect: ( ..a x [..a -- ..b] -- ..b x )
#
# Think of it as: "run this quotation, but protect the top value."
#
# Examples:
#   10 5 [ 1 i.+ ] dip          → ( 11 5 )
#   "world" "hello" [ ] dip     → ( "hello" "world" )
#
# Without dip, you'd need swap/rot gymnastics to work on
# values buried under other values.
#
# Your task: Use dip to add 10 to the SECOND value on the stack
# while preserving the top value.
# When done, delete the marker line below.

# I AM NOT DONE

: test-dip ( -- )
    # Stack starts: ( 20 5 )
    # Goal: add 10 to 20 (the second value), keep 5 on top
    # Result should be: ( 30 5 )
    # Use: [ 10 i.+ ] dip
    20 5
    # Your code here
    drop 0

    # Do not edit below this line
    # Top should be 5
    5 test.assert-eq
    # Second should be 30
    30 test.assert-eq
;
